---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import audit from '@/data/invoice-generator-audit-2026.json';

const { summary, rows } = audit;
const others = rows.filter((row) => row.publisher_own_tool === 'no');
const ours = rows.filter((row) => row.publisher_own_tool === 'yes');
const ordered = [...others, ...ours];

const yesNo = (value: string) =>
  value === 'yes' ? 'Yes' : value === 'no' ? 'No' : 'Not measured';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: 'Free invoice generator audit 2026',
  description:
    'Measurements of ' + summary.third_party_tools + ' free invoice generators: whether text typed into the ' +
    'page is sent to a server, how many third-party domains each page contacts, and whether a PDF can be ' +
    'downloaded without an account.',
  url: 'https://invoiceworkshop.com/research/free-invoice-generator-audit-2026/',
  license: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Invoice Workshop', url: 'https://invoiceworkshop.com/' },
  datePublished: summary.drawn_on,
  distribution: [{
    '@type': 'DataDownload',
    encodingFormat: 'text/csv',
    contentUrl: 'https://invoiceworkshop.com/research/free-invoice-generator-audit-2026.csv',
  }],
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Free invoice generator audit 2026 — what these tools do with what you type"
  description={'We measured ' + summary.third_party_tools + ' free invoice generators: does what you type leave the browser, who receives it, and how many other companies are on the page. Method and data included.'}
  path="/research/free-invoice-generator-audit-2026/"
  schema={schema}
>
  <article class="simple-page">
    <p class="eyebrow">Research · {summary.drawn_on}</p>
    <h1>What free invoice generators do with what you type</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      An invoice carries a customer's name, their address and what they were charged. We measured{' '}
      {summary.third_party_tools} free invoice generators to find out where that text goes when you
      type it: whether it leaves the browser, who receives it, and how much of the page belongs to
      companies other than the tool.
    </p>

    <p>
      Every figure below is an observation, not a rating. There is no score, because a score is
      only someone's weighting argued in public. The method is published, the raw data is
      downloadable, and anyone can re-run it.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>This is published by one of the tools in the table.</strong><br />
      Invoice Workshop is measured on exactly the same terms as everything else and appears in
      the table, but it is excluded from every figure quoted on this page. It did not rank in the
      top 20 for any of the {summary.queries} queries used to draw the sample — it is in the
      table only because we put it there, and that is worth knowing before you read further.
    </div>

    <h2>What we found</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>Most of them keep it in the browser.</strong> Of the {summary.measurable} tools where
      the test ran to completion, {summary.kept_in_the_browser} never sent what we typed anywhere.
      That is the opposite of what we expected before running it, and it is the most useful thing
      on this page.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>{summary.typed_data_left_the_browser} sent it to a server.</strong> For{' '}
      {summary.sent_only_to_the_tools_own_service} of those the recipient was the tool's own
      service, which is how a server-rendered PDF has to work and is not a criticism of anything.{' '}
      {summary.sent_to_an_unrelated_company} sent something to a company that was not the tool:{' '}
      {summary.unrelated_recipients.join(' and ')}. In both cases it was one field, not the
      invoice, and the table names which one — because the difference between an email address in
      a marketing form and a customer's address on an invoice line is the whole distinction, and a
      study that blurs it is worthless.
    </p>

    <p>
      Precisely: on invoicesimple.com the field labelled <em>Business Number</em> appeared in a
      request to <code>api-iam.intercom.io</code>, a third-party customer-messaging service. The
      rest of that invoice — business name, client name, street, item description, notes, both
      email addresses — went to <code>data.getinvoicesimple.com</code>, which is a different
      registered domain but carries the tool's own name. On xero.com's invoice template page, an
      email address typed into the form on the page reached <code>api.unifyintent.com</code>, a
      business visitor-identification service. No invoice content was involved in that one.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>The exposure is the advertising, not the invoice.</strong> The median tool loaded
      code from {summary.third_party_domains_median} other companies' domains while an invoice was
      being filled in. The highest was <strong>{summary.third_party_domains_max}</strong>, on{' '}
      {summary.third_party_domains_max_domain} — which was the first result for "free invoice
      generator" when the sample was drawn. None of those companies received the invoice. All of them were told you were on a
      page about invoicing, which is what an ad auction is for.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Almost nobody makes the promise.</strong> Only {summary.claimed_local_processing} of{' '}
      {summary.third_party_tools} say anywhere on their site that your data stays in your browser.
      Of those, {summary.claimed_local_but_sent_it_anyway} broke the claim. The claim is rare, not
      unreliable.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>{summary.not_measurable} of the {summary.third_party_tools} pages that rank are not
      generators.</strong> They are
      template galleries and landing pages: there is no invoice to fill in on the page you arrive
      at. They are listed as not measured rather than assumed either way, and they are worth knowing
      about before you click.
    </p>

    <h2>How the sample was drawn</h2>
    <p>
      The one place a comparison is normally rigged is deciding who is in it. Nobody decided here.
      On {summary.drawn_on} we ran {summary.queries} search queries — the phrases people actually
      type, fixed in advance and published in the data file — took the top 20 results for each,
      removed app stores, forums and articles about tools, and kept one page per domain. That gave
      {summary.third_party_tools} tools. No tool was added or dropped after the measurements were seen.
    </p>

    <h2>Method</h2>
    <p>
      Each tool was opened in a fresh browser with no history, no extensions and no stored data.
      Every network request the page made was recorded. A unique string —
      <code>AUDITCANARY7Q3X</code> — was typed into every text field on the page, the way a
      person filling in an invoice would. The main action was then triggered: download, generate,
      export, in that order of preference.
    </p>
    <p>
      Afterwards, every request the page had made since the typing started was searched for that
      string, in the URL and in the request body. If it appears, the text left the browser, and
      the table records which domain received it. If it does not appear, it did not leave during
      the test. That is the whole measurement — there is no judgement in it.
    </p>
    <p>
      A destination on a different registered domain is not automatically a different company.
      <code>invoicesimple.com</code> posts to <code>getinvoicesimple.com</code>; calling that a
      third party would be a false accusation about a company's own backend. The rule used here is
      mechanical: if the two names contain each other, they are counted as the same operator. It
      is a statement about the names, not about who owns what.
    </p>
    <p>
      Separately, the page's own words were searched for a claim of local processing — phrasings
      like "never leaves your browser", "processed locally", "client-side". Pairing that with the
      network result is what makes the claim column meaningful. A tool that makes no such claim and
      processes your invoice on a server is not failing anything; it is being straightforward.
    </p>

    <h2>What this does not show</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        It is one session per tool on one date. A tool that kept your data in the browser then can
        change tomorrow, and this does not watch it.
      </li>
      <li>
        Only the free flow a visitor gets was exercised — fill in the fields, click the main
        button. Anything behind an account, and anything that happens when you email an invoice
        from inside the tool, was never triggered and is not measured.
      </li>
      <li>
        "Did not leave the browser" means the canary was not found in any request this test saw.
        It is not an audit of a company, its servers, or how long it keeps anything.
      </li>
      <li>
        The PDF column records whether a file actually downloaded during the test. Tools that open
        a print dialog, render a preview, or email the PDF instead will read "No" here despite
        giving you a usable invoice. Do not read that column as "no free PDF".
      </li>
      <li>
        Third-party domain counts move with ad auctions and consent state. They measure how much of
        the page is other people's code, not a fixed property of the tool.
      </li>
      <li>
        The name-in-PDF column reports whether the tool's own name appears in the PDF's text. A
        footer credit and a stamped watermark both trip it; it cannot tell them apart.
      </li>
      <li>
        The harness fills every visible text field, so on a page that mixes an invoice editor with
        a newsletter box it will type into both. That is why the table names which field left
        rather than only that something did.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The data</h2>
    <figure class="doc-table-wrap">
      <figcaption>
        {summary.tools_in_frame} free invoice generators, measured {summary.drawn_on}
      </figcaption>
      <div class="doc-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Audit results table">
        <table class="doc-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Tool</th>
              <th scope="col">Typed data left the browser</th>
              <th scope="col">Sent to</th>
              <th scope="col">Which fields</th>
              <th scope="col">Claims local processing</th>
              <th scope="col">PDF file downloaded in the test</th>
              <th scope="col" class="numeric">Third-party domains</th>
              <th scope="col">Own name in the PDF</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {ordered.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">
                  {row.domain}
                  {row.publisher_own_tool === 'yes' && <span> — this site</span>}
                </th>
                <td>{yesNo(row.typed_data_left_the_browser)}</td>
                <td>{row.received_by || '—'}</td>
                <td>{row.fields_that_left || '—'}</td>
                <td>{yesNo(row.claims_local_processing)}</td>
                <td>{yesNo(row.pdf_downloaded_without_account)}</td>
                <td class="numeric">{row.third_party_domains_contacted}</td>
                <td>{row.own_name_in_pdf_text ? yesNo(row.own_name_in_pdf_text) : '—'}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="doc-table-note">
        Blank cells mean the measurement was not made, not that the answer was no. The full
        dataset carries the measured URL, timestamp, the exact claim quoted from each page, the
        button that was clicked and any notes.
      </p>
    </figure>

    <h2>Take the data</h2>
    <p>
      <a class="primary-link" href="/research/free-invoice-generator-audit-2026.csv" download>
        Download the full dataset (CSV)
      </a>
    </p>
    <p>
      Published under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" rel="license">CC BY 4.0</a>.
      Use it, quote it, re-run it, correct it.
    </p>

    <h2>How to cite this</h2>
    <p>
      Invoice Workshop, <em>Free invoice generator audit {summary.drawn_on.slice(0, 4)}</em>,{' '}
      {summary.drawn_on}.
      <br />
      <code>https://invoiceworkshop.com/research/free-invoice-generator-audit-2026/</code>
    </p>

    <h2>Found a mistake?</h2>
    <p>
      If your tool is in the table and the measurement is wrong, tell us what to re-run and we
      will re-run it and change the row. Write to <a href="/contact/">contact</a>. Corrections are
      the point of publishing the method.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* A URL and a hostname are single unbreakable words. At 360px the citation
     block pushed the whole page sideways, which the mobile-overflow test in CI
     is there to catch. */
  .simple-page code {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    word-break: break-word;
  }
</style>
